{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>When you sit to dine with a ruler, consider well what is before you (Proverbs 23:1).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, warns in this verse (Proverbs 23) concerning a man that he should not pursue after princes and after those appointed in the king's house — who is the ruler of the land — that he should not draw too near to him lest he be harmed, for kingship is likened to fire, which encompasses both benefit and harm: benefit in its warmth and light, harm to one who draws too near to it, for it burns him. And so our Sages, of blessed memory, warned concerning this and said: ״ואל תתודע לרשות״ — 'and do not make yourself known to the authority.' And kingship is called רשות (authority) because the authority and the power are in his hand to do anything — to open, to close, to save, and to destroy. And they further said: ״הוו זהירין ברשות״ — 'Be careful with the authority,' for they do not draw a person near except for their own need; they appear as friends at the time of their enjoyment but do not stand by a man in his time of distress. Besides, other harms befall him through this, for willy-nilly he will grow slack in the statutes of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His service, out of fear of the king, and will strive first in the king's statutes. Therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 23:26 — cf. 24:21): ״ירא את ה' בני ומלך עם שונים אל תתערב״ — 'Fear HaShem, my son, and the king; with those who change do not mix' — meaning that one should strive first in the fear of HaShem and afterward in the fear of the king, and not reverse the matter so as to strive first in the king's statutes and make them the essence, for this would be a profanation of the Name. And so we find in the Shunammite woman, who was a wise woman, as it is written (II Kings 4:8): ״ועבר אלישע אל שונם ושם אשה גדולה״ — 'And Elisha passed to Shunem, and there was a great woman' — that is, distinguished; and when Elisha said to her, ״היש לדבר לך אל המלך או אל שר הצבא״ — 'Is there anything to speak for you to the king or to the captain of the army?' — she answered him, ״בתוך עמי אנכי יושבת״ — 'I dwell among my own people.' Therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, said here: כי תשב ללחום את מושל — when you wish to draw near to the king's house to be among those who eat at his table, you should consider those who were before you already, in times past, who drew near to him — what became of them? For not everyone who reaches the king reaches benefit, and not everyone who trades profits. And this is what the verse says (Proverbs 29:26): ״רבים מבקשים פני מושל ומה' משפט איש״ — 'Many seek the face of a ruler, but a man's justice is from HaShem' — meaning that those who seek are many, but those who succeed in their seeking are not many, and all is in the justice of HaShem. And if you are a person of expansive soul, pursuing after virtues, you should refrain from this, lest that physical bread be pleasant to you; and this is what is written after it (Proverbs 23:2): ״ושמת שכין בלועך אם בעל נפש אתה״ — 'And put a knife to your throat, if you are a man of appetite.' This is the way of the plain sense (peshat)." "<b>By way of reason (sekhel):</b> This verse speaks of the kingship of heaven, and it is a warning that a man should not draw near in his apprehension beyond what is apprehensible, for the Holy One, blessed be He, is called in Scripture מושל (ruler), as David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 22:29): ״כי לה' המלוכה ומושל בגוים״ — 'For the kingship is HaShem's, and He rules over the nations.' And therefore it says: כי תשב ללחום את מושל בין תבין את אשר לפניך — when you sit to apprehend the wisdom, which is the nourishment of the soul just as bread is the nourishment of the body, and you wish to apprehend the apprehension of the Ruler, may He be exalted — בין תבין את אשר לפניך — understand Him from what is before you, that is, from His ways and wondrous deeds that are visible and manifest to the senses, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 8:4): ״כי אראה שמיך מעשה אצבעותיך״ — 'When I see Your heavens, the work of Your fingers' — this is בין תבין את אשר לפניך. And the doubling בין תבין means: understand after understanding, for the Creator, may He be exalted, is not apprehended at the first thought, but only after one thinks and returns and thinks again, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 4:39): ״והשבות אל לבבך כי ה' הוא האלהים״ — 'And you shall return to your heart, that HaShem, He is Elohim.' And so we find with Elijah (I Kings 19:11–12): ״ורוח גדולה וחזק וגו' לא ברוח ה'״ — 'And a great and strong wind… not in the wind of HaShem'; ״ואחר הרוח רעש לא ברעש ה'״ — 'and after the wind, an earthquake — not in the earthquake of HaShem'; ״ואחר הרעש אש לא באש ה'״ — 'and after the earthquake, fire — not in the fire of HaShem'; ״ואחר האש קול דממה דקה״ — 'and after the fire, a still small voice.' Therefore the expression is doubled, בין תבין, because it is a matter that requires contemplation after contemplation. And if you are a person of rational soul, you should refrain from apprehending beyond what is apprehensible; this is: ״ושמת שכין בלועך אם בעל נפש אתה״ — 'And put a knife to your throat, if you are a man of appetite' — for the jaws are an organ encompassing both eating and speech, and it is well known that whoever does not refrain from this and wishes to strain his thought on what is not apprehensible will perish and be desolate, in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 7:16): ״ואל תתחכם יותר למה תשומם״ — 'And do not be overly wise — why should you be desolate?' For so we find with the sons of Aaron, who were punished because they strove at the standing at Mount Sinai to apprehend beyond what is apprehensible — ״וחזו את האלהים״ — 'and they saw Elohim' — and they pressed to ascend to HaShem after Moses had warned them by the mouth of the Almighty (Exodus 19:24): ״והכהנים והעם אל יהרסו״ — 'And the priests and the people shall not press forward.' And because they drew near before HaShem too much, death was decreed upon them, and this is what is written." "<b>After the death of the two sons of Aaron, when they drew near before HaShem and they died.</b> This utterance was to Moses after the two sons of Aaron had died. And the sense of בקרבתם לפני ה' וימותו — 'when they drew near before HaShem and they died' — its construal is: when they ministered before HaShem, they died. Thus explained the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>By way of the plain sense (peshat):</b> it seems to me that this verse explains two transgressions that were in their hand: one in thought, for which death was decreed upon them, and the second in deed, in which they died. The sin of thought was their drawing near before HaShem at Mount Sinai after Moses had warned them, and for it they incurred death — this is what our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Exodus 24:11): ״ואל אצילי בני ישראל לא שלח ידו״ — 'And upon the nobles of the children of Israel He did not stretch out His hand' — from here, that they deserved the stretching out of a hand, but were delivered for the moment, so as not to mix up the joy of the Torah; and this is the expression בקרבתם, and it did not say בהקריבם (when they offered). The sin of deed in which they died is the offering of strange fire, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 10:1): ״ויקריבו לפני ה' אש זרה״ — 'And they offered before HaShem a strange fire'; and the strange fire was that they brought fire without incense. And the construal of the verse will be: אחרי מות שני בני אהרן וימותו — 'After the death of the two sons of Aaron, and they died' — after death had been decreed upon the two sons of Aaron for the sin of thought in their drawing near before HaShem at Mount Sinai, when their eyes feasted on the Shekhinah; and they died on the first of Nisan, for the sin of deed in offering strange fire. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses in utterance and in speech that he should tell Aaron to warn him not to enter the Holy at any time, within the veil, except with incense, in the manner of what is written in the parashah: ״והביא מבית לפרכת ונתן את הקטרת על האש לפני ה' וכסה ענן הקטרת את הכפרת ולא ימות״ — 'And he shall bring from within the veil and place the incense on the fire before HaShem, and the cloud of incense shall cover the mercy-seat, and he shall not die' — if so, if he entered there without incense he would die, in the manner that his sons died." "<b>And behold,</b> their sin is explicit, and the pericah testifies to this sin in the matter of their entering there without incense, and therefore he warns Aaron concerning this. And what strengthens this interpretation for you is what they said in the Midrash (Numbers 3:4): ״וימת נדב ואביהוא לפני ה׳ בהקריבם אש זרה לפני ה׳ במדבר סיני״ — 'And Nadab and Abihu died before HaShem when they offered strange fire before HaShem in the wilderness of Sinai.' Now, did they die in the wilderness of Sinai? What does the verse teach by saying 'before HaShem in the wilderness of Sinai'? Rather, it was 'before HaShem' — from Mount Sinai they took their warrant for death." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> אחרי מות שני בני אהרן — 'After the death of the two sons of Aaron.' Four sons of Aaron were liable to die because of the sin of the calf, and Moses prayed for them and his prayer achieved half. What is written there? (Deuteronomy 9) ובאהרן התאנף ה׳ מאד להשמידו — 'and HaShem was very angry with Aaron to destroy him.' And 'destruction' means the cutting off of children, as it says (Amos 2) ואשמיד פריו ממעל ושרשיו מתחת — 'and I will destroy his fruit from above and his roots from beneath.' Since Moses prayed, his prayer achieved half. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Did I not write in My Torah (Exodus 22) ״על כל דבר פשע על שור על חמור על שה על שלמה על כל אבדה אשר יאמר כי הוא זה עד האלהים יבא דבר שניהם אשר ירשיעון אלהים ישלם שנים לרעהו״ — 'for every matter of trespass, for ox, for donkey, for sheep, for garment, for every lost thing about which one says, This is it — the matter of both parties shall come before Elohim; he whom Elohim shall condemn shall pay double to his neighbor.' על כל דבר פשע — for every matter of trespass. על שור — for ox: this one is liable to punishment who transgressed with an ox, as it says (Psalms 106) וימירו את כבודם בתבנית שור — 'they exchanged their glory for the likeness of an ox.' על חמור — for donkey: these are the Egyptians about whom it is written (Ezekiel 23) אשר בשר חמורים בשרם — 'whose flesh is the flesh of donkeys,' and they are the mixed multitude who converted and went up with them from Egypt and were the cause of the calf. על שה — for sheep: these are Israel, who are called sheep, as it says (Jeremiah 50) שה פזורה ישראל — 'Israel is a scattered sheep.' For the mixed multitude began the matter of the calf and Israel attached themselves to them. על שלמה — for garment: even the great ones among them, about whom it is written (Isaiah 3) שמלה לכה קצין תהיה לנו — 'you shall be a garment for us, O ruler.' על כל אבדה — for every lost thing: for they were stray sheep in the wilderness and sought a leader, as it says (Jeremiah 50) צאן אובדות היו עמי — 'my people were lost sheep.' אשר יאמר כי הוא זה — about which one says, This is it: for they said (Exodus 32) אלה אלהיך ישראל — 'these are your gods, O Israel,' and it is written (ibid.) כי זה משה האיש — 'for this Moses, the man.' עד האלהים יבא דבר שניהם — the matter of both parties shall come before Elohim: this is Moses, about whom it is written (Exodus 7) ראה נתתיך אלהים לפרעה — 'see, I have made you Elohim to Pharaoh' — they came before Moses and he judged them. אשר ירשיעון אלהים — he whom Elohim shall condemn: this is Aaron, whom Moses — who is called Elohim — condemned, saying to him (Exodus 32) מה עשה לך העם הזה כי הבאת עליו חטאה גדולה — 'What did this people do to you that you have brought a great sin upon them?' ישלם שנים לרעהו — shall pay double to his neighbor: these are the two sons of Aaron who died, as it is written אחרי מות שני בני אהרן — 'after the death of the two sons of Aaron.' This is what the verse says (Ecclesiastes 9): ״הכל כאשר לכל מקרה אחד לצדיק ולרשע לטוב ולטהור ולטמא ולזובח ולאשר איננו זובח כטוב כחוטא הנשבע כאשר שבועה ירא״ — 'All things come alike to all; one fate happens to the righteous and to the wicked, to the good and to the clean and to the unclean, to him who sacrifices and to him who does not sacrifice; as is the good, so is the sinner; he who swears as he who fears an oath.' Solomon, peace be upon him, would look and gaze across all generations at the righteous and the wicked, and see things that happen to the wicked and also happen to the righteous, and he said: this too is evil in all that is done under the sun — one fate happens to the righteous and to the wicked. לצדיק — to the righteous: this is Abraham, as it says (Genesis 18) כי ידעתיו למען אשר יצוה את בניו ואת ביתו אחריו — 'For I have known him, to the end that he may command his children and his household after him.' ולרשע — and to the wicked: this is Nimrod, who incited the whole world to rebel against the Holy One, blessed be He. This one died and that one died — hence one fate to the righteous and to the wicked. לטוב — to the good: this is Moses, as it says (Exodus 2) ותרא אותו כי טוב הוא — 'and she saw him, that he was good.' ולטהור — and to the clean: this is David, as it says (I Samuel 16) וישלח ויביאהו והוא אדמוני עם יפה עינים וטוב רואי — 'and he sent and brought him, and he was ruddy, with fair eyes and good to look upon.' לטמא — and to the unclean: this is Nebuchadnezzar. David founded the Temple and reigned forty years; Nebuchadnezzar destroyed it and reigned forty years — hence one fate to all. ולזובח — and to him who sacrifices: this is Solomon, as it says (I Kings 8) ויזבח שלמה את זבח השלמים אשר זבח לה׳ בקר עשרים ושנים אלף — 'and Solomon sacrificed sacrifices of peace-offerings which he sacrificed to HaShem: twenty-two thousand oxen.' ולאשר איננו זובח — and to him who does not sacrifice: this is Jeroboam, who prevented Israel from going up on pilgrimage to the Temple, as it says (I Kings 12) רב לכם מעלות ירושלים — 'it is too much for you to go up to Jerusalem.' This one reigned after that one. כטוב — as is the good: this is Moses, as it says (Exodus 2) ותרא אותו כי טוב הוא. כחוטא — so is the sinner: these are the spies, about whom it is written (Proverbs 13) חטאים תרדף רעה — 'evil pursues sinners.' Moses did not enter the land; the spies did not enter the land. הנשבע — he who swears: this is Zedekiah, about whom it is written (II Chronicles 36) וגם במלך נבוכדנצר מרד אשר השביעו באלהים ועורו את עיניו — 'and he also rebelled against King Nebuchadnezzar, who had made him swear by Elohim, and he put out his eyes.' כאשר שבועה ירא — as he who fears an oath: this is Samson, as it says (Judges 15) השבעו לי — 'swear to me,' and it is written (Judges 16) ויאחזוהו פלשתים וינקרו את עיניו — 'and the Philistines seized him and put out his eyes.' Another interpretation: כטוב כחוטא — as is the good, so is the sinner. כטוב — as is the good: these are the sons of Aaron. כחוטא — so is the sinner: this is Korah and his congregation, who quarreled with Moses and were burned, as it says (Numbers 16) ואש יצאה מאת ה׳ ותאכל — 'and fire came forth from HaShem and consumed.' The sons of Aaron, who entered to offer sacrifice, were burned, as it says (Leviticus 10) ותצא אש מלפני ה׳ ותאכל אותם — 'and fire came forth from before HaShem and consumed them' — hence one fate to all. And of this Elihu said (Job 37) אף לזאת יחרד לבי ויתר ממקומו — 'At this also my heart trembles and leaps from its place.' And if dry wood that the Holy One, blessed be He, left alive in this world afterward sprouted and brought forth fruit and came out alive, while the sons of Aaron entered alive and came out burned — 'at this also my heart trembles and leaps from its place.' This is what the verse says (Ecclesiastes 2): לשחוק אמרתי מהולל ולשמחה מה זו עושה — 'I said of laughter, It is mad, and of joy, What does it do?' Rabbi Aḥa said: Solomon said things over which the attribute of justice laughed at him. It is written (Deuteronomy 17) ולא ירבה לו נשים — 'he shall not multiply wives for himself,' and it is written (I Kings 11) ויהי לו נשים שרות שבע מאות ופילגשים שלש מאות — 'and he had seven hundred royal wives and three hundred concubines.' And it is written (Deuteronomy 17) לא ירבה לו סוסים — 'he shall not multiply horses for himself,' and it is written (I Kings 5) ויהי לשלמה ארבעים אלף ארוות סוסים — 'and Solomon had forty thousand stalls of horses.' It is written (Deuteronomy 17) וכסף וזהב לא ירבה לו מאד — 'silver and gold he shall not greatly multiply for himself,' and it is written (I Kings 10) ויתן המלך את הכסף בירושלים כאבנים — 'and the king gave silver in Jerusalem like stones' — silver was accounted as nothing in the days of Solomon. ולשמחה מה זו עושה — 'and of joy, what does it do?' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: What is this crown in your hand? Step down from your throne. At that hour an angel came in the likeness of Solomon and sat upon his throne, and Solomon would go about crying out in every synagogue and every house of study, saying to them: 'I am Qohelet; I was king!' And they would strike him with a reed and set before him a bowl of legumes. At that hour he said: זה היה חלקי מכל עמלי — 'this was my portion from all my toil.' Another interpretation: לשחוק אמרתי מהולל — this is the laughter that the attribute of justice laughed over Elisheba daughter of Amminadav, who saw four joys in one day: she saw her brother-in-law as king, her husband as high priest, her brother as prince, and her two sons as deputies of the priesthood. When they entered to offer sacrifice they came out burned — hence לשחוק אמרתי מהולל. This is what the verse says (Job 39:27–28): אם על פיך יגביה נשר וכי ירים קנו, סלע ישכון ויתלונן על שן סלע ומצודה, משם חפר אוכל למרחוק עיניו יביטו, ואפרוחיו יעלעו דם ובאשר חללים שם הוא — 'Is it at your command that the eagle mounts up, and makes his nest on high? He dwells on the rock and lodges upon the crag of the rock and the stronghold. From there he seeks food; his eyes behold it from afar. And his young ones gulp blood; and where the slain are, there is he.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Job: אם על פיך יגביה נשר — 'is it at your command that the eagle mounts up?' If at the utterance of your mouth I would cause My Shekhinah to dwell upon the Ark, just as I did through Aaron, and at the utterance of your mouth I would remove My Shekhinah from upon the Ark. סלע ישכון ויתלונן — 'he dwells on the rock and lodges': this is the First Temple. על שן סלע ומצודה — 'upon the crag of the rock and the stronghold': this is the Second Temple. And He calls 'rock' the Foundation Stone (אבן שתיה) that was there before the Ark. And why is it called אבן שתיה? For from it the world was founded (הושתת). משם חפר אוכל — 'from there he seeks food': from within that Foundation Stone the high priest would pray for Israel for the supply of their sustenance, saying: May it be Your will, HaShem our Elohim, that this coming year be upon us for rain and dew, satiety — a year of plenty, a year of favor, a year of blessing, a year of trade; a year in which the house of Israel need not depend one upon another for sustenance, nor upon any other people, and that no one shall raise dominion over another. למרחוק עיניו יביטו — 'his eyes behold it from afar': for he would know what would be at the end of the year. How so? If the smoke rose in the arrangement toward the south — great plenty for the people of the south; if the smoke rose toward the west — great plenty for the people of the west; and likewise for the east and likewise for the north; if it rose straight up toward the firmament, he knew that the whole world was sated. All this ascent and all this praise belonged to Aaron, and nevertheless ואפרוחיו יעלעו דם — 'and his young ones gulp blood,' meaning his children would gulp blood in silence; rather, ובאשר חללים שם הוא — 'and where the slain are, there is he' — there the Shekhinah was. Thus far in the Midrash." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> אחרי מות שני בני אהרן וימותו — 'After the death of the two sons of Aaron, and they died.' It is possible to interpret that the duplication of 'dying' is like the matter of (Deuteronomy 33:6) יחי ראובן ואל ימות — 'Let Reuben live and not die' — as Onkelos renders it. And furthermore, the verse testifies (Numbers 3:4) ובנים לא היו להם — 'and they had no sons,' and you already know that one who dies without children falls within the scope of this matter, and included in the Torah's allusion is what is written (Leviticus 22:13) ובת כהן כי תהיה אלמנה וגרושה וזרע אין לה ושבה אל בית אביה כנעוריה מלחם אביה תאכל — 'And the daughter of a priest, if she be a widow or divorced and have no seed, and return to her father's house as in her youth, she shall eat of her father's bread.' He calls the soul 'the daughter of a priest,' for it flows forth from Binah by way of the right side, which is Ḥesed. And he says: if she be a widow — from the body that has been lost to her; and divorced — in that she does not return to her root, since she has no seed; and after the days of divorce are completed for her, in that she is banished from the place where she is fit to return, ושבה אל בית אביה כנעוריה מלחם אביה תאכל — 'and she shall return to her father's house as in her youth; of her father's bread she shall eat' — which is Kodesh, that is to say, she shall enjoy the radiance of the Shekhinah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {2 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:59.425317512Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}